From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:51:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Let the guest network use a user-mode stack instead of NAT

Virtualization.framework's NAT attachment is implemented by macOS
Internet Sharing, which turns on IP forwarding and loads its own pf
ruleset on the host, including `set skip on lo0`. That breaks hosts that
rely on pf for loopback traffic, such as transparent proxying.

Add a `network` config section. Mode "nat" (the default) is unchanged.
Mode "unixgram" connects the guest's network device to a user-mode
network stack over a Unix datagram socket, using vfkit's protocol, so
gvproxy's `-listen-vfkit` can provide DHCP, DNS and outbound NAT without
touching the host's network configuration.

Also capture `self` weakly in the outer closure of VsockProxy's connect,
which the inner `[weak self]` relied on; Swift warned that the outer
strong capture made the inner weak one ineffective.
